            <ArticleTitle>Increased adhesiveness of white blood cells in patients with unstable angina: additional evidence for an involvement of the immune-inflammatory system.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1017-20</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Data are being accumulated on the presence of inflammatory response in patients with acute coronary syndromes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="HYPOTHESIS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The study was undertaken to confirm that the adhesive state of the peripheral blood leukocytes can provide information on an inflammatory process in patients with unstable angina pectoris.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The study included 21 patients with unstable angina, 32 patients with stable angina, and 13 patients with chest pain and no evidence of coronary artery disease.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The percentage of aggregated leukocytes in patients with unstable angina (5.8 +/- 3.2%) was significantly (p &lt; 0.02) increased compared with that of patients with no evidence of active coronary disease (3.2 +/- 2.6%).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Additional evidence for an existing smoldering inflammatory process in unstable angina pectoris can be provided by testing the percentage of aggregated leukocytes in peripheral blood.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
